     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n\r\n</font><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 8pt\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Calibri\" size=\"3\">Hello all,</font></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 8pt\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n\r\n</font><p style=\"margin:0in 0in 8pt\"><font face=\"Calibri\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\">I have a number of bird species for which I ran NMix models\r\nusing the pcount function.<span>  </span>Result from\r\nmy models were a mixed bag, partially stemming from low detectability.<span>  </span>I wish to report out the model fits (good and\r\nbad) but I was not quite clear on the best way to interpret the output from my goodness\r\nof fit tests (parboot, </font><span style=\"color:black\">Nmix.gof.test)</span><font color=\"#000000\">on the global models.<span>  </span>Through readings on this group and in the Applied\r\nHierarchical Modeling in Ecology test, I think I grasp the bullet points about\r\ndispersion and p-values, but was hoping someone here might be generous enough\r\nto offer some further clarity or point to some more resources.<span>  </span>Here are some example values from my GOF\r\noutputs:</font></font></font></p><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n\r\n</font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n\r\n</font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n  </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n  </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n  </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n  </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n </font><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">\r\n  </font><fon