   "subject": "Re: Underdispersion and GOF for Pcount Models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Cal,<div><br></div><div>Yes, p-vals approaching 1 reported by parboot and gof functions do indicate underdispersion and hat the fitted model cannot effectively reproduce your empirical data. </div><div><br></div><div>I don&#39;t know of any good rules of thumb regarding specific values for underdispersion, although I guess c-hat =0.5 is as extreme as c-hat = 2. To the best of my knowledge, this hasn&#39;t really been explored very deeply for these types of models. I don&#39;t think the tests distinguish between an underdispersed state process vs. obs. process, and I&#39;d blindly speculate that whether underdispersion matters kind of depends upon where it&#39;s happening. I dunno, some simulation (and, within your data, looking for patterns underlying the discrepancies) might be pretty useful.</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div><br></div><div>John</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
